New episodes of More Life every week, listen wherever you get your podcasts or watch on YouTube.
每周都有《更多生活》的新节目，在各大播客平台收听或在 YouTube 上观看。

00:30:51.710 --> 00:30:55.259
End of commercials, back to show.
广告结束，回到节目。

00:30:55.259 --> 00:31:04.099
Well, I love that an episode, a more serious episode has these bursts of hilarity in them.
嗯，我很喜欢一集相对严肃的剧集中夹杂着这种爆笑的桥段。

00:31:04.099 --> 00:31:18.500
Or conversely, you have these episodes that are largely comedy-driven that really, oh, hi, Nelson, like really shocks you, really shocks you almost with the gravitas of it.
或者反过来，那些原本以喜剧为主的剧集，真的——哦，嗨，纳尔逊——真的震撼到你，几乎以它的凝重感震撼了你。

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:32.500
But I also think it goes right into the vein of a pretty important theme of how I met your mother, which is how much do I honor or work on myself versus how much do I work on partnering?
但我也认为，这直接触及了《老爸老妈浪漫史》中一个非常重要的主题，那就是：我究竟该花多少精力去成就或完善自我，又该花多少精力去经营伴侣关系？

00:31:32.500 --> 00:31:35.119
How much do I give up of myself within a partnership?
在亲密关系中，我又该放弃多少自我？

00:31:35.119 --> 00:31:40.160
How much do I fight to hold on to my individuality or these other dreams?
我该如何争取保留我的个性或这些其他的梦想？

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:47.880
How much of I'm willing to sacrifice in regards to making a relationship work or looking for one?
为了维系一段感情或寻找伴侣，我愿意牺牲多少？

00:31:47.880 --> 00:32:00.950
And I think that I thought this was so smart because Ted is the one who seems like he's an upheaval in the unknown, and from the get-go, Marshall and Lily are the settled ones.
我觉得这个设定非常聪明，因为泰德看起来一直在未知中颠沛流离，而从一开始，马歇尔和莉莉就是安定的一对。

00:32:00.950 --> 00:32:07.549
the question of who they're going to be with is settled and they are settling with each other, that looks like, well, that was a done deal.
他们和谁在一起的问题已经尘埃落定，他们选择了彼此，看起来就像是一桩板上钉钉的事。

00:32:07.549 --> 00:32:11.230
Like Robin says, everyone wants what you have, right?
正如罗宾所说，每个人都渴望拥有你们的生活，对吧？

00:32:11.230 --> 00:32:18.529
And I thought it was, again, like the, what, how I make your mother does best, which is, it's not all one thing.
我又觉得，这正是《老爸老妈浪漫史》最擅长的，那就是：事情从来都不是单一的。

00:32:18.529 --> 00:32:34.599
Nothing is ever one thing, you know, and I, and I think we mistrust, you know, it's almost like, like, like how a wife guys on, on social media get a lot of shit because they're like, they're always talking about how great their wife is and how great their marriage is.
没有任何事情是单纯的一面，你知道的，而且我觉得我们往往会心存怀疑，你知道的，这几乎就像，就像社交媒体上的那些模范丈夫经常挨骂一样，因为他们总是大谈特谈自己的妻子有多好，婚姻有多美满。

00:32:34.599 --> 00:32:40.640
It's like it's always they're gonna have an affair like within, you're gonna get exposed within the year.
这就好像他们迟早会出轨一样，一年之内就会露馅。

00:32:40.640 --> 00:32:49.240
Like I think we've learned to mistrust anything that does not paint with like more colors.
我觉得我们已经学会了不去信任任何色彩单一的事物。

00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:55.519
Yeah, and there's this interesting switch that's happening where Ted and Raman are starting to have this detente and trouble is brewing from Marshall and Lily.
是的，这里发生了一个有趣的转变，泰德和罗宾开始走向缓和，而马歇尔和莉莉却开始出现麻烦。

00:32:55.519 --> 00:32:59.079
And I, that's a very important kind of the pads crossing.
而且，这是一种非常重要的命运交错。

00:32:59.079 --> 00:33:03.160
And it's kind of these two different wavelengths kind of crossing there.
这就像是两种不同的频率在那里交汇。

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:05.640
And yeah, nothing is all one thing, right?
是的，没有什么事情是完全单一的，对吧？

00:33:05.640 --> 00:33:09.079
And even Lily says, I'm not having doubts about Marshall.
甚至连莉莉也说，我对马歇尔没有怀疑。

00:33:09.079 --> 00:33:11.119
I'm having doubts about me.
我对我有怀疑。

00:33:11.119 --> 00:33:13.400
And that's that idea of you're in your late 20s.
这就是那种你处于二十多岁末期的感觉。

00:33:13.400 --> 00:33:15.640
And are you fully you yet?
你真的完全成为你自己了吗？

00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:17.480
Has the cement dried on you?
你身上的水泥已经干透定型了吗？

00:33:17.480 --> 00:33:18.759
Are you an adult yet?
你已经是个成年人了吗？

00:33:18.759 --> 00:33:20.880
Are you the thing you thought you were going to be?
你成了你曾经以为你会成为的那种人了吗？

00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:27.960
Is this the moment you should be most chasing your dream or most realizing like in real life, you don't do every single dream.
现在应该是你最该追逐梦想的时候，还是最该认清现实的时候——在现实生活中，并不是每一个梦想都能实现。

00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:36.465
you know, you trade off, you trade one thing for another or you adjust your dream to include this other person and settling now with them.
你知道的，你会有所权衡，用一件事去换取另一件，或者调整你的梦想，把另一个人纳入其中，并与他们安定下来。

00:33:36.465 --> 00:33:44.476
And she's really tried having that late 20s crisis that I think so many people have that I think we all probably have in some way.
她真的经历那种许多人都有的、二十多岁末期的危机，我觉得我们所有人可能都在某种程度上经历过。

00:33:44.476 --> 00:33:49.838
I don't I really don't feel like I was a full grown up to like maybe yesterday morning.
直到差不多昨天早上，我才真的觉得自己完全成年了。

00:33:49.838 --> 00:33:54.769
It's just like you're late 20s, you're still you're still so in progress, right?
就好像二十快到头的时候，你其实还是处在不断成长中，对吧？

00:33:54.769 --> 00:33:58.097
I mean, you're in progress your whole life, hopefully.
我的意思是，希望你这一生都在不断成长。

00:33:58.097 --> 00:34:05.862
But Lily's really having a moment here and it really plants a seed that's going to inform the end of the season in a huge way.
但莉莉在这里确实面临一个重要时刻，这也埋下了一个伏笔，将对本季的结局产生重大影响。

00:34:05.862 --> 00:34:08.266
It's also a question about partnership.
这也是一个关于伴侣关系的问题。

00:34:08.266 --> 00:34:11.471
I think there's two active forces in why we partner.
我认为我们结为伴侣有两个主要的推动力。

00:34:11.471 --> 00:34:13.320
One, we meet the right person.
第一，我们遇到了对的人。

00:34:13.320 --> 00:34:16.821
Two, it's the right time in our life to kind of do it.
第二，在我们的人生中，这个时候做这件事刚刚好。

00:34:16.821 --> 00:34:33.551
And sometimes I feel, especially for people who I felt like my older sister married her college boyfriend, they didn't get together until their junior year, but she met him on the first day of like on the orientation bus, like the first year orientation bus.
有时候我觉得，特别是对于那些——我觉得我大姐嫁给了她的大学男友，他们直到大三才在一起，但她在第一天，比如在迎新大巴上就遇到他了，就像大一迎新大巴那样。

00:34:33.551 --> 00:34:35.886
And they were friends for two years.
然后他们做了两年朋友。

00:34:35.886 --> 00:34:39.452
And they've been married for years and years and years.
接着他们结婚很多很多年了。

00:34:39.452 --> 00:34:48.660
And I don't know if she ever questioned that, but sometimes I think when you meet someone so young, you could be like right person wrong time.
我不知道她是否曾对此产生过疑问，但有时我觉得，当你这么年轻就遇到某个人时，你可能会觉得是对的人但时间不对。

00:34:48.660 --> 00:34:57.739
Like it's too early, but this is the right person for me, then I think you have to engage in a process of, what's the word, like, co-growth?
就好像时间太早了，但如果这确实是对的人，那么我认为你必须经历一个过程，怎么说呢，类似于共同成长？

00:34:57.739 --> 00:35:05.909
Like, you're growing with someone or alongside someone, and the hope is that you're growing somewhat directionally, similarly.
就像，你在和某个人一起成长，或者说并肩成长，而你希望你们的成长方向在某种程度上是相似的。

00:35:06.550 --> 00:35:14.599
Because a lot of times you can, one, you know, I've heard it said that relationships where two people are committed to growth can work.
因为很多时候，其一，我听说过这样一种说法，两个人都有意愿成长的情感关系是能维系下去的。

00:35:15.400 --> 00:35:23.639
Relationships where neither are committed to growth also can work, but a relationship where one person is committed to growing and the other is not will not work.
两个人都对成长不上心的关系也能维持，但是，如果一个人致力于成长而另一个人没有，这种关系就无法长久。

00:35:24.199 --> 00:35:25.942
Yeah, that's that's right.
对，确实是这样。

00:35:25.942 --> 00:35:29.159
Yeah, I think Lily is having a crisis of timing.
对，我觉得莉莉遇到的是时间上的危机。

00:35:30.039 --> 00:35:34.002
She's not like she even says, I'm not having a crisis about the man.
她甚至不是在——正如她自己所说，我不是对这个人感到危机。

00:35:34.002 --> 00:35:35.809
I'm having a crisis about time.
我对的是时间感到危机。

00:35:35.809 --> 00:35:39.014
And I think like this is one of the things about aging.
我觉得这就是变老带来的感受之一。

00:35:39.014 --> 00:35:58.303
Now, I didn't start feeling this until I was approaching forty, but there is a feeling, I think, when you age, especially as you as you enter into middle age, whereas, and this is something I was exploring in my movie liberal arts, but when you're in college, or high school, or even just out of college, everything feels possible.
好吧，我直到快四十岁时才开始有这种感觉，但随着年龄增长，特别是当你步入中年时，确实会有这样一种感觉——这也是我在我的电影《大学同学》里探讨过的主题——当你还在上大学、高中，或者刚毕业时，一切皆有可能。

00:35:58.303 --> 00:36:07.044
Like, like, there's so many doors you could walk through, you know, you could pivot, you could be like, oh, I studied this, but I now want to do this.
就像，有那么多条路你可以选择，你知道的，你可以转变方向，你可以想，哦，我学的是这个，但我现在想做那个。

00:36:07.044 --> 00:36:09.491
That that is still all kind of of allowed.
那种状态在当时基本上都是被允许的。

00:36:09.491 --> 00:36:28.139
But by the time you hit or approaching 40, even sometimes your mid 30s, you've made choices that have real consequences to them, whether you had kids, you've lived a long time in a certain city, you've had a career that's taken you a certain amount of distance, might be really hard to leave and pivot into a new career.
但等你到了或者快到四十岁，甚至有时在三十岁中期，你做出的选择已经带有真正的后果了，无论你是生了孩子，在一个特定的城市住了很久，还是拥有了一段让你前行了相当距离的职业生涯，要离开并转向一条新的职业道路可能会非常艰难。

00:36:28.139 --> 00:36:41.950
So I think that one of the things if you're going to talk about aging, and I think because how I met your mother ran for so long, we ended up dealing with aging, maybe even more specifically than you guys had set out to at the beginning.
所以我觉得，如果要谈论变老这件事，而且鉴于《老爸老妈浪漫史》播了那么久，我们最终探讨衰老这个话题，可能比你们一开始计划的还要具体得多。

00:36:41.950 --> 00:36:49.701
But one of the big questions around aging in time is we don't have all the time in the world to live all the stories we want to live.
但关于衰老和时间的重大问题之一，就是我们没有无限的时间去经历我们想经历的所有故事。

00:36:49.701 --> 00:36:55.995
We can't marry everyone we're attracted to or everyone we have some feeling of chemistry or connection with.
我们不可能和所有吸引我们的人结婚，也不可能和所有让我们产生化学反应或心电感应的人在一起。

00:36:55.995 --> 00:36:57.451
That would be impossible.
那是不可能的。

00:36:57.451 --> 00:36:59.141
We'd need multiple lifetimes.
我们需要好几辈子的时间。

00:36:59.141 --> 00:37:05.960
So we have to make these choices and when you make choices, you naturally door snap shot slap, you know, a slam shot.
所以我们必须做出这些选择，而当你做出选择时，自然而然地，大门就会砰地关上，你知道的，砰然关上。

00:37:05.960 --> 00:37:18.920
And there's a, it's, um, well, I don't know if I've talked about this, but, um, deer sugar, um, uh, Cheryl Strade, who is, if you haven't read, um, uh, tiny, beautiful things, have you read that book either of you?
而且有一种，它是，嗯，我不知道我有没有说过这个，但是，亲爱的糖果，嗯，切丽·斯特雷德，如果你还没读过，嗯，《微小的小事》，你们两个谁读过那本书吗？

00:37:18.920 --> 00:37:19.920
It's incredible.
那书太棒了。

00:37:19.920 --> 00:37:20.920
No, I haven't read it.
没有，我没读过。

00:37:20.920 --> 00:37:21.920
Incredible.
太棒了。

00:37:21.920 --> 00:37:26.199
But she, she wrote Wild and she also had this advice column called Deer Sugar.
但她，她写了《荒野》，她还主持过一个叫“亲爱的糖果”的咨询专栏。

00:37:26.199 --> 00:37:45.039
And one of her most beautiful letters is about a guy, he's entering his late 30s, he's with a woman, they're both around the same age, they're both incredibly happy, they don't want to be with anyone else, they want to stay together, they don't know if they should have kids or not, they could make equally compelling arguments about having kids or not having kids.
她最美的信件之一是写给一个男人的，他快要到三十岁末期了，他和一个女人在一起，两人年龄相仿，都极其幸福，不想和别人在一起，想要长相厮守，但不知道该不该要孩子，对于要不要孩子他们都能说出同样充分的理由。

00:37:45.039 --> 00:37:51.159
So they're really at this like fork in the road kind of crisis moment and they're like we have to decide.
所以他们真的处于这种十字路口的危机时刻，他们觉得必须做个决定。

00:37:51.159 --> 00:38:04.000
And so the guy writes her and turns her and she said, you will make a choice and you'll be standing on the shore one day of looking out at the sea.
于是这个男人写信给她并向她倾诉，她回答说：你将做出一个选择，总有一天你会站在海岸边，望向大海。

00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:06.480
And she calls it the ghost ship that didn't carry us.
她把这叫做“那艘没载着我们的幽灵船”。

00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:14.559
Like often the distance they'll be this ship going by with your other life, the other choice that you didn't make, you know.
就像在远方，会有一艘船驶过，载着你的另一种生活，也就是你没有做出的那个选择，你知道的。

00:38:14.559 --> 00:38:17.960
And you kind of just stand on the shore of your own choice and your own life.
而你只能站在属于你自己的选择和生活的海岸上。

00:38:17.960 --> 00:38:26.280
And you have to salute that ship and you have to grieve on some level that you're not on that ship because there's heartbreak either way, you know?
你必须向那艘船致意，并且在某种程度上为自己不在那艘船上而感到悲伤，因为无论选哪条路都会心碎，你知道吗？

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:38.599
And I think that's something, there are concessions, there are trade-offs, every big decision we make is laced with a kind of grief and a kind of relief and a kind of celebration.
我觉得这就是生活，其中包含着妥协和权衡，我们做出的每一个重大决定都交织着某种悲伤、某种解脱和某种庆祝。

00:38:38.599 --> 00:38:43.960
So it's just a, I think she's asking herself the biggest questions you could be asking.
所以这只是一种，我觉得她正在问自己你能问出的最重大问题。

00:38:43.960 --> 00:38:44.960
They're appropriate questions.
这些都是很契合的问题。

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:51.079
Well, look, I mean, at the very beginning of this episode, we saw Lily almost die when Alec almost killed her.
你看，我是说，在这个剧集的开头，我们看到莉莉差点死掉，因为亚历克差点把她害死。

00:38:51.079 --> 00:38:55.199
So her whole life flashed before her into a deep existential crisis.
所以她的一生在她眼前闪过，引发了一场深刻的存在主义危机。

00:38:55.199 --> 00:38:57.559
I could have died because of Alec Love.
我居然因为亚历克·爱（Alec Love）差点死掉。

00:38:57.559 --> 00:39:02.599
No, but I think one thing I like about Lily's journey in this one is like, she's been the rock for everybody.
不，不过我觉得莉莉在这集里的心路历程有一点很好，那就是，她一直是每个人的精神支柱。

00:39:02.599 --> 00:39:03.599
You know what I mean?
你懂我的意思吗？

00:39:03.599 --> 00:39:05.719
She's always everybody's rock.
她一直都是所有人的主心骨。

00:39:05.719 --> 00:39:06.719
She's Marshall's rock.
她是马歇尔的主心骨。

00:39:06.719 --> 00:39:08.679
She's the moral center of this group.
她是这个团队的道德核心。

00:39:08.679 --> 00:39:12.400
We've seen her spend the whole season kind of being the moral grounding of this group.
我们看到她一整季都在某种程度上扮演着这个团队的道德基石。

00:39:12.400 --> 00:39:14.800
I'm so struck by that on this rewatch.
这次重看让我对此印象深刻。

00:39:14.800 --> 00:39:17.199
Really is the moral center of this group.
她确实是这个团队的道德核心。

00:39:17.199 --> 00:39:22.760
She is the person that everyone looks to for gravity and the north star and the compass.
她就是所有人寻找的那种稳定力量、北极星和指南针。

00:39:22.760 --> 00:39:29.429
And she and this one is realizing, I've been that for everybody for a long time.
然后她以及这个角色意识到：“我这么长时间以来一直是大家的依靠。”

00:39:29.429 --> 00:39:39.070
I feel like I rushed into things a little more than I had planned when I broke up with my high school boyfriend who was trying to make me marry him and go to umpire school with him and live in Staten Island.
我觉得自己当初有点冲动，在跟高中男友分手时做决定比计划中快了点，那个男生当时想逼我跟他结婚、去上裁判学校，还要跟我一起住在史泰登岛。

00:39:39.070 --> 00:39:57.309
Now I found this wonderful man, Marshall, who I do love, and is the right guy, but it feels all a little and fast forward and something about moving that wedding date up by many, many months and putting that piece, additional piece into fast forward, makes you realize like my whole 20s have been a little bit in fast forward.
现在我找到了这个绝佳的男人——马歇尔，我是真的很爱他，他也是对的人，但这一切感觉都有点像按了快进键，而且把婚礼日期提前了整整好几个月，把这个额外的决定也按下了快进键，这让你意识到——我整个二十几岁的人生其实都像是在按快进键。

00:39:57.309 --> 00:39:59.070
I've always been the grounded one.
我一直都是那个最稳重的人。

00:39:59.070 --> 00:40:00.510
When do I get to freak out?
那我什么时候才能崩溃一次呢？

00:40:00.510 --> 00:40:03.190
And the answer is in the next episode.
答案就在下一集里。

00:40:03.190 --> 00:40:14.190
And it's very human and Lily gets to be human and flawed in a way that I think really dimensionalizes her because no one is always the grounded gravitational center.
而且这非常人性化，莉莉终于有机会展现出人性以及不完美的一面，我觉得这让她的形象真正变得立体了，因为没有人能永远做那个沉稳的重力中心。

00:40:14.190 --> 00:40:15.989
She's a human being too.
她也是个凡人。

00:40:15.989 --> 00:40:22.670
Well, sometimes, we do this in our family, but I think we also do this in friend groups or are chosen or horizontal family.
嗯，有时我们在自己的家庭里会这样，但我认为我们在朋友圈或者我们选择的“水平家庭”里也是如此。

00:40:22.670 --> 00:40:24.869
We fall into these roles.
我们会陷入这些固定的角色中。

00:40:24.869 --> 00:40:36.349
And Lily, from the moment this show started, plays the role of the mother hen, the wise, the moral center, the moral compass.
而莉莉从这部剧一开始，就扮演着老母鸡的角色，扮演着那个睿智的、道德核心、道德指南针的角色。

00:40:36.349 --> 00:40:39.190
she's the voice of reason often.
她经常是理智的声音。

00:40:39.190 --> 00:40:42.150
And it's almost like she's rebelling against that role.
这感觉就像她正在对那个角色进行反叛。

00:40:42.150 --> 00:40:46.030
Like, wait a minute, I actually don't know as much.
就好像是：“等等，其实我没懂那么多。”

00:40:46.030 --> 00:40:54.469
And I think it's a great truth that we can often see so clearly other people's lives and what they need to do more than we can see our own.
我觉得这是一个很棒的真理：我们往往比看清自己更能看清别人的生活以及他们需要做什么。

00:40:54.469 --> 00:40:56.829
We have a deep blind spot when it comes to ourselves.
在面对我们自己时，我们有着很深的盲区。

00:40:56.829 --> 00:41:01.630
So I think she just reaches the end of, I've got it all figured out, I know.
所以我想她只是到了一个临界点，觉得自己“一切都想明白了，我都懂”。

00:41:01.630 --> 00:41:08.750
And she just, it's almost like a, I don't know, it was like a time release capsule, this kind of crisis that she was just needing to have.
然后她就——这感觉就像是一个定时释放的胶囊，她正需要这样一场危机。

00:41:08.750 --> 00:41:20.789
Yeah, and she gets to have, and she writes, and the way it's gonna throw a bomb into this whole group when they're moral center, when they're gravity, that's all holding them on the surface of the earth when she freaks out.
对，而且她确实迎来了这场危机，当莉莉情绪崩溃时，这就好比往整个团队中扔了一颗炸弹，因为她是他们的道德核心，她是他们的重力，把所有人紧紧吸附在地球表面。

00:41:20.789 --> 00:41:26.909
There's a great moment in Jaws where they bring in the shark expert guy, and it's like, ah, good, we got the shark expert guy.
《大白鲨》里有一个非常精彩的桥段，他们把那个鲨鱼专家请了过来，心想：啊，太好了，我们有鲨鱼专家了。

00:41:26.909 --> 00:41:28.230
He's the shark hunting expert.
他是鲨鱼猎捕专家。

00:41:28.230 --> 00:41:31.590
He's gonna get the shark, and then he dies, and the shark gets him.
他会把鲨鱼抓住的。结果他死了，鲨鱼把他吃了。

00:41:31.590 --> 00:41:35.446
It's like, fuck if the shark expert guy's gone, really fucked here.
这感觉就像是：操，如果鲨鱼专家都没了，那我们可真他妈完蛋了。

00:41:35.446 --> 00:41:42.642
It's a little bit like Lily is about to sort of get eaten by like the late 20s shark here and kind of have her own freak out.
这有点像莉莉即将被二十几岁末期的“鲨鱼”吃掉，并经历她自己的崩溃。

00:41:42.642 --> 00:41:48.917
And it's I like how it casts everybody a little bit a little bit out of our gravitational pull in season two.
而且我喜欢第二季里这种设定，它把每个人都稍微推出了我们的引力范围。

00:41:48.917 --> 00:41:50.990
And that's what season two is about.
这就是第二季的主题。

00:41:50.989 --> 00:41:55.352
I'm not I'm about I got one foot in kind of like astrology, whatever.
我并不是——我其实对星座之类的东西半信半疑。

00:41:55.352 --> 00:41:58.134
And then the other foot's like I don't know.
另一半心里又觉得：我不知道。

00:41:58.134 --> 00:42:10.399
But there is something called the Saturn Returns, which is this idea that from when in your 27 to 29, somewhere in there, the planets kind of realign of how they were roughly when you were born.
但确实有“土星回归”（Saturn Returns）这种说法，这个概念指的是当你在27到29岁左右的某个时候，行星的运行会重新对齐，大致回到你出生时的位置。

00:42:10.399 --> 00:42:45.300
So people say in those years, 27 to 29, which is literally the first three seasons of how I met your mother, one of two things happens, either where you've been working and what you've been doing kind of gathers some steam and you feel some wind at your sails, like things start, things start really moving because you were on a path that You put pager dues, and doors start flying open, or you realize you've been on, your ladder's been parked up against the wrong wall, and you have to climb down and put it up against a wall that feels more aligned.
所以人们说在那几年里——也就是27岁到29岁，这正好是《老爸老妈浪漫史》的前三季——会发生两件事中的一件：要么是你一直以来的工作和努力开始积蓄势头，你会感到顺风顺水，事情开始真正向前发展，因为你一直走在正确的路上，各种大门纷纷为你敞开；要么你就会意识到自己一直以来都走错了路，你的梯子搭错了墙，你必须爬下来，把它重新搭在一面感觉更契合的墙上。

00:42:45.300 --> 00:42:59.940
So it almost feels a lot of, you could, you could do, I'm sure someone has done this, you could do a kind of astrological breakdown of the Saturn returns of all these characters and what's going on for them.
所以感觉你可以——我相信肯定有人这么做过——你可以对所有这些角色的“土星回归”以及他们各自经历的事情做一个星象学层面的剖析。

00:42:59.940 --> 00:43:05.920
It feels, it feels like it's, that, that, that feels, Stay tuned for our bonus episode where we just do that.
这感觉——这感觉——敬请收听我们的加更集，我们在那一集里就会专门做这个。

00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:08.059
We'll be getting the astrologer on here to break that down.
我们会请占星师来节目里为我们详细解读。

00:43:08.059 --> 00:43:20.289
Okay, well this, we are now at a beloved segment of the show, which we call questions and observations from a clinical psychologist who's never seen how I met a mother and also happens to be married to Josh.
好的，那么我们现在来到了节目的备受喜爱的环节，我们称之为“从未看过《老爸老妈浪漫史》的临床心理学家的提问与观察，她同时也是乔什的妻子”。

00:43:20.289 --> 00:43:30.610
My favorite part of this episode was when Lily was exploring the road not taken, the life that was in certain ways sacrificed when she met Marshall and made the choice to be with him.
这一集我最喜欢的部分是，莉莉探索了“未选择的路”，也就是她遇到马歇尔并选择和他在一起时，在某种程度上牺牲掉的人生。

00:43:30.610 --> 00:43:38.130
It reminds me of something I often say to patients, which is that with every choice, even the good ones, there's grief.
这让我想起我经常对病人们说的一句话：伴随着每一次选择，即使是好的选择，也会有悲伤。

00:43:38.130 --> 00:43:43.730
Because whenever you say yes to one thing, you're also simultaneously saying no to all the other options.
因为每当你对一件事说“好”的时候，你同时也对所有其他的选项说“不”。

00:43:43.730 --> 00:43:50.329
So I'm wondering if you can identify with that feeling, perhaps in your careers, and if so, when.
所以我很好奇你们是否能对这种感觉产生共鸣，也许是在你们的职业生涯中，如果有的话，是在什么时候？

00:43:50.329 --> 00:44:06.849
I also wanna note that this was another episode sort of like belly full of turkey that highlighted that despite the show being about looking for love and the idealization of finding that person through Marshall and Lily's relationship, you wisely don't let us stay in that fantasy.
我还想指出，这就像是另一集类似于《满肚子的火鸡》的剧集，它凸显了尽管这部剧是关于寻找爱以及通过马歇尔和莉莉的关系来将“找到那个人”理想化，但你们很明智地没有让我们一直停留在那种幻想中。

00:44:06.849 --> 00:44:14.369
For too long, you show us that people in good and strong relationships have their own struggles too, which I really appreciate.
你们没有持续太久，而是向我们展示了处于良好且牢固关系中的人也有他们自己的挣扎，我真的很欣赏这一点。

00:44:14.369 --> 00:44:15.730
I love that point.
我很喜欢这个观点。

00:44:15.730 --> 00:44:17.730
That is, yeah, that's what this was about.
就是这样，对，这就是这一集的内核。

00:44:17.730 --> 00:44:19.090
That's what this episode was.
这就是这一集要表达的。

00:44:19.090 --> 00:44:30.610
You know, Alec, I meant to talk to you about this, that we should probably play Jordana's questions a little earlier in the episode because she always points out, by the time you get to my question, you've covered a lot of what I've said.
你知道的，亚历克，我本来想跟你谈这个，我们或许应该在节目稍微早一点的时候播放乔丹娜的问题，因为她总是指出，等你们聊到我的问题时，你们已经把我要说的大部分内容都覆盖了。

00:44:30.610 --> 00:44:38.650
So she, and it is true, but I will say this, she'll say to me, why are you taking all my stuff?
所以她——而且确实如此，但我得这么说，她会对我说：“你为什么抢我所有的梗？”

00:44:38.650 --> 00:44:42.809
And I will be able to say to her, I hadn't heard your question because I hadn't listened to this one.
而我就可以对她说：“我根本没听过你的问题，因为我还没听这段录音呢。”

00:44:42.809 --> 00:44:46.130
Yeah, no, I love the surprise of not hearing a prayer.
对，没错，我喜欢这种没听过提问的惊喜感。

00:44:46.130 --> 00:44:58.610
Yeah, I think we're both, I think she even more eloquently than I did points up the fact that every choice is laced with grief and with sacrifice.
对，我觉得我们俩——我觉得她比我更口吐莲花、更精准地指出了这样一个事实：每一个选择都交织着悲伤与牺牲。

00:44:58.610 --> 00:45:14.849
But I do think her second point is lovely and well-taken which is anytime, and again, we did touch on it a little bit, but anytime we idealize a relationship, what is it like, don't compare your insides to other people's outsides?
但我觉得她的第二个观点很棒，也非常中肯，那就是无论何时——再次强调，我们之前其实也稍微触及过这一点——每当我们把一段关系理想化时，那句话怎么说来着，“不要拿你看到的别人的表面生活来和你的内心情感作比较”？

00:45:14.849 --> 00:45:28.090
Like if we really jumped into another relationship that we looked like, that we idealized or another career that we idealized, we would spend like five minutes and go, you know what, I'll take my own problems back.
就像如果我们真的盲目跳进另一段我们所羡慕的、被我们理想化的关系中，或者进入另一份我们所理想化的职业里，我们大概只会花个五分钟就会说：“你知道吗，我还是把自己的老问题拿回去吧。”

00:45:28.090 --> 00:45:33.889
Like, we're not, it's so tempting to think someone has it better than we do.
就像我们不会——总觉得别人过得比我们好，这种想法太诱人了。

00:45:35.010 --> 00:45:41.489
But everyone has their own battles they're fighting in no matter what they project out into the world.
但无论他们在世界上展现出怎样光鲜的外表，每个人都有属于他们自己的战斗要打。

00:45:41.489 --> 00:45:50.489
Yeah, and yeah, this show, without saying too much about future seasons, like we play with the idea in this show about the idea of the one, right?
对，而且——对，这部剧在不过多剧透未来几季的前提下，我们在剧中探讨了“真命天子”这个概念，对吧？

00:45:50.489 --> 00:45:56.449
The idea of like, idealizing any one thing, any one outcome, does that fit?
关于理想化任何单一事物、任何单一结局的想法，这真的合适吗？

00:45:56.449 --> 00:45:58.449
Is that realistic, right?
这现实吗，对吧？

00:45:58.449 --> 00:46:00.809
There's a lot of twists and turns to life.
生活有太多的曲折和转折。

00:46:00.809 --> 00:46:04.769
Even when you find your person, there's a journey, right?
即使你找到了你的真爱，这也是一段旅程，对吧？

00:46:04.769 --> 00:46:14.239
And life finds a way to challenge that and to make a different narrative than purely the one you had planned for yourself.
生活总会找到方法去挑战这一点，并创造出一个与你原本为自己纯粹规划好的剧本截然不同的故事。

00:46:14.239 --> 00:46:16.719
And I think we see that here with Marshall and Lillian.
我想我们在马歇尔和莉莉身上看到了这一点。

00:46:16.719 --> 00:46:17.880
And I think that's honest.
我认为这很真诚。

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:18.719
I think that's honest.
我认为这很真诚。

00:46:18.719 --> 00:46:20.199
I think that's very real.
我认为这非常真实。

00:46:20.199 --> 00:46:23.639
And I think Lily was due for this, right?
而且我觉得莉莉早就该经历这一遭了，对吧？

00:46:23.639 --> 00:46:27.760
She's been a grownup, but the more we explore Lily later in series, she's been the grownup.
她一直表现得像个成年人，但随着我们在剧集中对莉莉进行更深入的挖掘，她一直都是那个成熟的大人。

00:46:27.760 --> 00:46:29.840
She was kind of the grownup with her parents a little bit.
在面对她父母时，她多少有点像个大人。

00:46:29.840 --> 00:46:31.199
She was the grownup with scooter.
在面对斯库特时，她是个大人。

00:46:31.199 --> 00:46:32.719
She's grown up with Marshall.
她在马歇尔面前也是个成熟的大人。

00:46:32.719 --> 00:46:37.519
And she needed a moment to not have to be under that level of gravity.
她需要一个时刻，不用再承受那种级别的重压。

00:46:37.519 --> 00:46:39.480
And I think it's really interesting.
我觉得这真的很有意思。

00:46:39.480 --> 00:46:41.880
These next couple of episodes are really interesting for Lily.
接下来的这几集对莉莉来说真的非常有趣。

00:46:41.880 --> 00:46:43.880
There's some great Ted and Lily stuff coming up in the next one, Josh.
乔什，下一集里有一些泰德和莉莉的很棒的对手戏。

00:46:43.880 --> 00:46:47.840
I think you guys were really great together in the next episode we'll talk about.
我觉得我们在接下来要讨论的那一集里，你们两个的合作真的很棒。

00:46:47.840 --> 00:46:53.119
And yeah, I think challenging that idea of perfection and anyone's at the finish line.
而且，对，我觉得挑战那种完美主义的概念，以及“谁已经冲过终点线”的想法。

00:46:53.119 --> 00:46:54.679
No one's at their finish line, right?
没有人冲过终点线，对吧？

00:46:54.679 --> 00:47:00.719
You're always, there's all the life is one big marathon and you're not at the finish line really ever until the end of the end, end, end.
你一直在——生活是一场宏大的马拉松，直到生命的尽头、尽头、尽头，你才算真正到达终点。

00:47:00.719 --> 00:47:05.079
And Marshall and Lily, they weren't done.
而马歇尔和莉莉，他们还没结束。

00:47:05.079 --> 00:47:05.920
They weren't done.
他们还没结束。

00:47:05.920 --> 00:47:07.360
Nothing was all the way solved yet.
一切都还没有完全解决。

00:47:07.360 --> 00:47:09.599
You know, someone said something to me about parenting.
你知道的，有人曾跟我谈过育儿经。

00:47:09.599 --> 00:47:18.800
They said, parenting is particularly difficult because just when you know how to parent a three month old, they're six-month-old.
他们说，育儿之所以特别困难，是因为正当你刚弄懂怎么照顾三个月的婴儿时，他们就六个月大了。

00:47:18.800 --> 00:47:27.840
And just when you feel like you've got the hang of the one-year-old, they're three-year-old, three-years-old, which necessitates a whole other level of new information.
而正当你觉得自己摸清了带一岁小孩的门道时，他们三岁了，而三岁又需要一整套全新的知识储备。

00:47:27.840 --> 00:47:34.559
And it almost feels like Marshall and Lily were really good at being college sweethearts.
这感觉就像马歇尔和莉莉很擅长做大学情侣。

00:47:34.559 --> 00:47:41.840
And then they got really good at being boyfriend and girlfriend who lived together with Ted in New York City.
然后他们变得很擅长做和泰德一起在纽约市合租的男友和女友。

00:47:41.840 --> 00:47:46.840
And then they had to adjust to being, and you saw Lily trying to adjust to that.
接着他们不得不适应——你看到莉莉试图去适应这一点。

00:47:46.840 --> 00:47:51.039
having the ring and what that means, and maybe she wouldn't be flirting anymore.
戴上戒指意味着什么，也许她以后再也不能和别人调情了。

00:47:51.039 --> 00:47:52.559
Guys wouldn't be approaching her.
男生们也不会再来搭讪她了。

00:47:52.559 --> 00:47:59.679
Then, oh my God, we're getting married in two months, and that's just a cold plunge that just shocks her system.
然后，天呐，我们两个月后就要结婚了，这就像是一场冷水澡，彻底给她的系统来了个大冲击。

00:47:59.679 --> 00:48:07.280
Yeah, and it really sets off like a little kind of ticking time bomb that takes, I really feel momentum at the end of this episode.
对，这真的像引发了一个小小的定时炸弹，让我在这一集的结尾真正感受到了情绪的推动力。

00:48:07.280 --> 00:48:12.280
The two of them dancing to their song and it's both sweet and ominous was really cool.
他们两个伴随着自己的歌声跳舞，那种画面既甜蜜又透着不祥的预感，真的很酷。

00:48:12.280 --> 00:48:17.280
I think in this otherwise very light feeling episode, them dancing to good feeling by the violent femmes.
我觉得在这一集原本非常轻松愉快的氛围中，他们随着暴力女性乐队（Violent Femmes）的《Good Feeling》翩翩起舞。

00:48:17.280 --> 00:48:21.000
And there's some, there's kind of like, there's kind of jaw, to bring it back to Jaws again.
而且——为了再次把话题扯回《大白鲨》——

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:25.599
There's kind of like a little Jaws music by playing underneath it, like emotionally.
这就有点像在情感暗流中，隐约播放着《大白鲨》的主题音乐。

00:48:25.599 --> 00:48:28.840
There's something I like, that's very interesting to watch.
我喜欢某些东西，看着非常有趣。

00:48:28.840 --> 00:48:30.199
I really liked that at the end of this.
我很喜欢这集的结尾。

00:48:30.199 --> 00:48:31.840
And it spins you into the end of the season.
它把你们带入了这一季的尾声。

00:48:31.840 --> 00:48:50.936
It's also the perfect song from the perfect band because violent femmes, even their name, like there is discordant, you know, like violent femmes is like, they're like toe-tapping, fun band who are also like, they've got a darkness to them and a dissonance to them and even singing about good feeling.
这也是来自完美乐队的完美歌曲，因为暴力女性（Violent Femmes），甚至他们的名字，你知道的，就有一种不和谐感，你知道，就像暴力女性这支乐队，他们听起来像是那种让人想跟着轻叩脚趾、很有趣的乐队，但同时他们身上又有一种黑暗感和不协和音，甚至连在唱好心情时也是如此。

00:48:50.936 --> 00:48:59.501
There's like these flat notes that feel like, you know, like, like, just like they won't quite let you have the good feeling, you know.
有那种听起来像是平音的音符，让你觉得，你知道的，就像是……就是不愿让你完全拥有那种好心情，你知道的。

00:48:59.501 --> 00:49:09.525
Do you have any moments, Craig, in your life that you felt like you knew you made the right choice, but you also felt the sting of the sacrifice every moment.
克雷格，你的人生中有没有过这样的时刻：你觉得自己做了正确的选择，但每一刻你也都感受到了牺牲带来的刺痛感？

00:49:09.525 --> 00:49:13.839
Probably a lot, probably a lot of them probably every moment, right?
可能有很多，可能有很多次，大概每个时刻都是吧？

00:49:13.840 --> 00:49:16.852
I mean, yeah, I mean, it's such a tough one to answer.
我是说，对啊，这真的很难回答。

00:49:16.852 --> 00:49:20.924
It's like, you sign up for things thinking you know what they are, right?
这就好比，你答应做一件事情时以为自己知道它是什么，对吧？

00:49:20.924 --> 00:49:24.550
And then they turn out to be complicated beyond what you thought.
然后结果却发现它比你想象的要复杂得多。

00:49:24.550 --> 00:49:28.832
And they're like, this wasn't exactly what I ordered up out of the catalog.
接着你会想：这可不是我从目录里点的那款商品啊。

00:49:28.832 --> 00:49:31.744
And then you have to figure out how it fits anyway.
然后你只能想办法不管怎样先适应它。

00:49:31.744 --> 00:49:38.309
And then any choice, you can't be haunted by that grass is always greener shit because you will never get anywhere.
而且在任何选择面前，你都不能被“草总是绿的”这种鬼话所纠缠，因为那样你将一事无成。

00:49:39.389 --> 00:49:43.670
Most of the big decisions I've made feel like the right ones and also they're challenging.
我做过的大多数重大决定都觉得是正确的，但同时也充满挑战。

00:49:43.750 --> 00:49:55.030
And they also have an adjustment period, you know, like when someone starts a new job or moves and they come to me kind of in there, scared, I say, give it four months, give it six months.
它们也需要一段适应期，你知道的，就像有人开始一份新工作或者搬家时，他们带着恐惧来找我，我会对他们说：给它四个月，给它六个月。

00:49:55.030 --> 00:50:00.349
Like if you're deciding in the first couple of days, you will want to leave, you'll be convinced you made the wrong decision.
如果你在头几天就做决定，你会想离开，你会坚信自己做错了决定。

00:50:00.349 --> 00:50:04.190
Like we have a metabolizing kind of grace.
就像我们需要有一种自我代谢式的宽容。

00:50:04.190 --> 00:50:06.550
we have to give ourselves.
我们必须给自己这种宽容。

00:50:06.550 --> 00:50:15.230
For me, I know that sometimes you get offered two jobs at once and you feel you got to decide.
对我来说，我知道有时你同时获得两份工作机会，你会觉得必须立刻做出抉择。

00:50:15.230 --> 00:50:19.909
I have a couple of passes that haunt me a little bit because it's a sliding door.
我有几次错过的机会至今仍让我有些萦绕心头，因为那就像一扇滑动门。

00:50:19.909 --> 00:50:28.389
It's like, well, what if I'd done that job instead of this one or what if I'd done that job instead of deciding to go away or do you know what I mean?
就像是，如果我选了那份工作而不是这一份会怎么样？或者如果我选了那份工作而不是决定离开或者别的什么，你知道我的意思吗？

00:50:28.389 --> 00:50:32.909
Showbiz gives you a lot of those moments of second-guessing and self-reflection.
演艺圈会给你很多这种充满自我怀疑和反思的时刻。

00:50:32.909 --> 00:50:33.909
Yeah.
对。

